    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Sealer lifespan is not really a property of the product. It is a property of how thick the film went down, what the sun and the freeze-thaw cycle do to it, and how many tyres turn on it — which is why two driveways sealed on the same day with the same pail can be two years apart in condition.
        </p>

        <div id="by-type" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Long Each Type of Sealer Lasts
          </h2>

          <p>
            The chemistry sets the ceiling. Within that ceiling, everything else decides where you land. These are realistic figures for a properly applied two-coat job on sound residential asphalt.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sealer Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Lifespan</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Strengths</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Trade-off</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Asphalt emulsion</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-3 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low odour, easier application, widely legal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Shorter cycle than coal tar</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Coal tar emulsion</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4-5 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Best chemical and fuel resistance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strong odour; restricted in some areas</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Oil-based / cutback</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-4 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Penetrates and rejuvenates dry asphalt</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Long cure, strong solvent smell</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Acrylic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-3 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Colour stability, works on concrete too</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lower chemical resistance, higher cost</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Filler-and-sealer combos</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-3 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Handles hairline crazing in one step</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thinner build than dedicated sealer</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Coal tar is the durability leader and has been for decades, because it cures to a harder, more chemically resistant film than asphalt emulsion. It is also restricted or banned in a number of states and municipalities over environmental concerns about polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbon runoff. Check local regulations before planning around a four to five year cycle, because in much of the country that option is simply not available.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Number That Matters Most</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Two coats or one changes lifespan more than product choice.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A two-coat asphalt emulsion job routinely outlasts a single-coat coal tar application, despite coal tar being the more durable chemistry. Film thickness is the dominant variable. If you are choosing between a premium product applied once and a mid-range product applied twice, apply twice.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="what-drives-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Five Variables That Decide How Long It Lasts
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two identical driveways sealed on the same afternoon can be in visibly different condition three years later. These are the reasons, roughly in order of impact.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">1. Film Thickness</h3>

          <p>
            The dominant factor. Sealer protects by being present, and it wears away from the top down. A two-coat application has roughly twice as much material to lose before bare asphalt appears. This is why the single most effective thing you can do for longevity costs nothing but a second day: apply the second coat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">2. Surface Preparation</h3>

          <p>
            A film that never bonded properly does not wear away gradually — it lifts. Sealer over dust adheres to dust. Sealer over an untreated oil stain does not adhere at all and peels in a patch within weeks. Preparation determines whether you get gradual wear over years or localised failure in months.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">3. Sun Exposure</h3>

          <p>
            Ultraviolet light degrades the binder in both the sealer and the asphalt beneath. A south-facing driveway with no shade will wear measurably faster than a north-facing or tree-shaded one in the same town. This is also why the driveway sections nearest the street, which usually get the most open sky, tend to go grey first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">4. Freeze-Thaw Cycling</h3>

          <p>
            Water that gets into or under the film freezes, expands and lifts. A climate with many crossings of the freezing point through the winter is harder on sealer than a consistently cold one, because it is the cycling rather than the cold itself that does the damage. Northern driveways generally need resealing sooner than the same product in a mild climate.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">5. Traffic and Turning</h3>

          <p>
            Straight-line driving wears sealer slowly. Turning wheels, particularly power steering turned while stationary, scrubs it off. The wear pattern on almost every driveway tells this story: the tyre tracks go grey first, and the spot where cars pivot into the garage goes grey first of all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="signs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How to Tell When Your Sealer Has Worn Out
          </h2>

          <p>
            Do not reseal on a calendar. Reseal when the surface tells you to, which is usually somewhere inside the range for your product type but rarely exactly on the anniversary.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>The colour has faded from black to a consistent grey across the main traffic areas, not just at the edges.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Individual aggregate stones are becoming visible and feel exposed underfoot, rather than sitting under a continuous film.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Water no longer beads or sheets off the surface and instead soaks in and darkens the asphalt.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>The tyre tracks are visibly lighter than the unused margins of the driveway.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Fine hairline cracks are beginning to appear in the surface that were not there last year.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            The water test is the most useful single check. Pour a bucket over a section in the main traffic lane. On a sound sealed surface the water sits and sheets off. On a worn one it darkens the asphalt immediately as it soaks in. Water absorption is exactly the failure mode sealing exists to prevent, so it is the right thing to measure.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Resist the Urge to Reseal Early</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Sealing before the film has worn thin causes buildup.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Fresh sealer over a film that is still intact stacks thickness that eventually becomes too rigid to flex with the asphalt, and then cracks and peels in plates. A driveway sealed every year for six years is usually in worse condition than one sealed twice in the same period. Wait for the grey.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="extend" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How to Get More Years Out of a Sealing Job
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most of the levers are pulled on application day, and they cost time rather than money.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-4 my-8">
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">1</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Wash, do not just sweep</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Bond strength is the foundation of longevity. A washed and fully dried surface bonds; a swept one traps a dust layer between the sealer and the asphalt.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">2</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Prime every oil spot</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Petroleum contamination prevents bonding entirely. Degrease, scrub, and use an oil spot primer on anything that has soaked in. These spots are where peeling starts.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">3</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Fill every crack first</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">An open crack lets water reach the base and undermines the film from below. Fill anything over an eighth of an inch and let it cure before sealing.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">4</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Apply two thin coats</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The highest-leverage step available. Twice the film means roughly twice the material to wear through before asphalt is exposed.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">5</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Seal in settled warm weather</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Above 50°F day and night, no rain for 24 to 48 hours. A coat that cured properly is far more durable than one that cured in marginal conditions.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">6</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Keep the surface clear year-round</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Sweep leaves and debris, clean spills promptly, and avoid metal snow shovel blades and plough edges that scrape the film off.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">7</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Do not turn the wheel while stopped</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Dry steering scrubs sealer off in exactly one spot. Roll slightly while turning into the garage and the pivot mark largely disappears.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            One genuine product decision affects longevity: where coal tar is legal and the odour during application is acceptable to you, it buys roughly an extra two years per cycle over asphalt emulsion. Over a decade that is one fewer sealing weekend. Where it is not legal, a two-coat emulsion job applied carefully in good weather is the realistic path to the top of the two to three year range.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-schedule" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Lifespan Is Not the Same as Resealing Interval
          </h2>

          <p>
            These two numbers get conflated constantly and they are different questions. Lifespan is how long the film survives before it stops protecting. Resealing interval is how often you should apply a new coat, and the right interval is slightly shorter than the lifespan — you want to reseal as the film wears thin, not after bare asphalt has been weathering for a season.
          </p>

          <p>
            For asphalt emulsion, that means a two to three year interval against a two to three year lifespan, with the specific year chosen by looking at the surface rather than the calendar. In a sunny, high-traffic, freeze-thaw climate you will be at the short end. On a shaded, lightly used driveway in a mild climate you may comfortably stretch past three years.
          </p>

          <p>
            The failure mode at each end is different and worth knowing. Resealing too early causes buildup, cracking and sheet peeling. Leaving it too late means the asphalt itself begins oxidising and ravelling, and once aggregate starts coming loose no amount of sealer restores it — you have moved from maintenance into repair. Of the two errors, leaving it slightly too late is the less damaging, but only slightly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="diy-vs-pro" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Does a Professional Job Last Longer Than DIY?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sometimes, and for reasons that are worth understanding rather than assuming, because the gap is smaller than most people expect and it narrows to nothing if you are willing to be thorough.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Professionals Have a Genuine Advantage</h3>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Equipment that applies material at a consistent rate, removing the variability of a hand-pulled squeegee.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Access to commercial-grade products with higher solids content than most consumer pails.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Experience reading a surface — recognising base failure that no sealer will fix, before applying anything.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Speed, which means the whole driveway is coated within one weather window rather than across a weekend.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where the Advantage Disappears</h3>

          <p>
            The variables that dominate lifespan — surface cleanliness, crack repair, two coats rather than one, and settled weather — are all available to a homeowner willing to spend the time. A careful DIY job with washed asphalt, primed oil spots, filled cracks and two thin coats applied in good conditions will comfortably outlast a rushed commercial job that sprayed a single thin coat over a swept surface on a marginal day.
          </p>

          <p>
            That rushed commercial job is not hypothetical. Volume sealcoating operations are paid by the driveway, and the incentive runs toward speed. If you hire out, the questions worth asking are whether the quote includes crack filling as a separate step, how many coats are included, and what the weather contingency is. A contractor who answers those specifically is selling a different product from one who quotes a flat price per square foot.
          </p>

          <p>
            The realistic summary: professional application removes execution risk and buys back a weekend. It does not buy a fundamentally longer-lived coat unless it comes with a better product or an extra coat than you would have applied yourself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            7. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging Lifespan by the Label Alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Coverage and durability figures on a pail describe a properly applied two-coat job on well-prepared, sound asphalt in good weather. Applied thinly over a dusty surface in marginal conditions, the same product delivers a fraction of that. The number is a ceiling, not a promise.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Resealing on the Calendar Instead of the Surface</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An annual reseal reminder stacks film on top of film that has not worn thin, and the accumulated thickness eventually becomes rigid enough to crack and lift in plates. Look at the driveway instead: reseal when the traffic lanes have gone consistently grey and water soaks in rather than beading.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying One Coat and Expecting Full-Term Life</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A single coat contains pinholes and thin spots by definition, and it wears through at the high points first. Two thin coats is the difference between the short end and the long end of any product’s stated range, and it matters more than which product you bought.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Oil Spot Primer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealer cannot bond over petroleum contamination. Every untreated oil stain becomes a spot where the coat lifts and peels within weeks, and those spots then admit water under the surrounding film. Degrease and prime them, or expect localised failure long before the rest of the driveway wears out.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Metal Shovel Through the Winter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A steel shovel blade or a plough edge dragged across sealed asphalt scrapes the film off in strips, concentrating years of wear into a single season. Plastic or poly-edged shovels and a raised plough blade preserve the coat that you spent a weekend applying.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does driveway sealer last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Asphalt emulsion sealer typically lasts two to three years, coal tar four to five, and oil-based cutback sealers three to four. Those figures assume a properly applied two-coat job on a well-prepared surface. A thin single coat over poor preparation can wear through in a single season.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does sealcoating last on a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A standard residential sealcoat lasts two to three years before it needs renewing. Sun exposure, freeze-thaw cycling and turning traffic all shorten it; shade, mild winters and light use extend it. The traffic lanes and the pivot point outside the garage always wear first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does coal tar sealer really last longer than asphalt emulsion?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, typically four to five years against two to three, because coal tar cures to a harder and more chemically resistant film. However, it is restricted or banned in a number of states and municipalities over environmental concerns, so check local regulations. A two-coat emulsion job also outlasts a single-coat coal tar application.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know when to reseal my driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Look for the traffic lanes fading to a consistent grey, individual aggregate stones becoming visible, and water soaking in rather than beading on the surface. The water test is the most reliable: pour a bucket in the main traffic lane and watch whether it sheets off or darkens the asphalt.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you make driveway sealer last longer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, mostly through application choices. Wash rather than sweep, prime every oil spot, fill all cracks first, apply two thin coats rather than one thick one, and seal in settled weather above 50°F. Through the year, avoid metal snow shovels and avoid turning the steering wheel while stationary.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it bad to seal a driveway too often?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Sealing before the previous film has worn thin stacks thickness that eventually becomes too rigid to flex with the asphalt underneath, and it then cracks in a fine web and peels away in plates. A driveway sealed annually for several years is usually in worse shape than one sealed on a two to three year cycle.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

