        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Expect two to three years from asphalt emulsion, four to five from coal tar where it is legal, and three to four from an oil-based cutback — but treat those as ceilings rather than promises. They describe a two-coat job on a clean, sound, crack-free surface applied in settled warm weather.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Film thickness is the variable that moves the number most, and it is entirely in your control. A two-coat emulsion application routinely outlasts a single coat of the more durable coal tar chemistry. If you take one thing from this page, take the second coat.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then let the driveway tell you when to reseal rather than the calendar. Grey traffic lanes, visible aggregate and water soaking in instead of beading mean the film has done its job and needs renewing. Resealing before that point does not add protection — it adds thickness, and thickness is what eventually cracks and peels.
          </p>
        </div>

